Chapter 12 — Don't Leave, Please

Sanwa Building, Yoyogi. Roof. 11:31 p.m.

Nine floors put her above the pachinko sign and below everything that mattered. The roof was tar and gravel and forty years of pigeon business, two condensers humming out of sync, an antenna mast bolted through the deck with rust bleeding down its guys in long orange tongues. The tar had spent the day cooking and was giving the heat back now in a slow exhale that came up through the concrete into the backs of her thighs. The breeze off the park came the other way, cool and green and faintly wet, and the two of them met somewhere around her ribs.

Minako sat on the parapet coping with her spine against the antenna's dead-man anchor and her legs out along the stone.

Her heels stood by the stairwell door where she'd stepped out of them, one tipped over on its side. The black silk had ridden up when she'd hauled herself onto the ledge and she hadn't fixed it, and she wasn't going to fix it. It lay across her hips in a slack diagonal, the hem caught high on her right thigh, and the lace under it was two fingers wide and matte black, and where her legs crossed at the ankle her bald pussy was covered by exactly as much fabric as the stylist had promised — which was to say the idea of fabric, the rumor of it. Her tits sat loose and full under the silk. The breeze had found her nipples ten minutes ago and stayed. The bow was in her fist, wound twice around two fingers.

Below her, Tokyo lay down and glittered. The park was a black hole punched in the middle of it, ringed with lamps, and past the hole Shinjuku's towers stood up out of the dark with their red aircraft lights blinking on a rhythm she'd stopped being able to unsee years ago. Somewhere south an ambulance ran its siren for four seconds and gave up.

She heard the stairwell door before it opened — the latch that never caught, the shoulder-shove that people who knew it used.

Minako did not turn her head. She had a whole vocabulary for this. She let her chin stay out toward the city, let her mouth go soft and unoccupied, dropped one shoulder so the strap slid a half-inch, and made every line of herself say I've been sitting here like this for an hour and I didn't hear you come in. It was the single most rehearsed thing she'd done all day, and the point of it was so that he would arrive somewhere restful.

The door banged.

"You're on the ledge," Byl said.

"I'm on the ledge."

"Nine floors."

"I know how many floors. I did eleven setups on this roof in July." She turned her face then, slow. "Hello."

He filled the doorway the way he'd filled the alley, six foot four of him, ducking out of habit under a header that had eight inches to spare. The dark shirt was blotched white across the chest and both forearms where he'd slapped the plaster dust off himself and made it worse — chalk handprints, one clean and one smeared, like something had grabbed him and let go. His boots were dark to the laces and left prints on the dry tar that faded behind him as he came. There was a fine gray line of ceiling in his eyebrow. He'd left the tool bag downstairs.

Lucy came out from behind his knee, gave the roof one professional sweep, and headed for Minako at a trot.

And Byl looked at her.

Her face first. That was the part she'd remember later, out of the whole night, and turn over, and fail to file. His eyes went to her eyes and stayed there a beat too long to be manners — asking, plainly, with nothing in it but the question — and whatever he found looking back gave him what he'd asked for.

Then he took her in.

He did it the way he'd read the violin on Michiru's roof. Bare feet, dirty on the soles from the stairs. The long line of shin and calf. The hem caught high and the lace under it and the shadow the lace wasn't covering. The waist. The silk over her tits and what the wind had done there. His eyes widened by a few degrees — a small mechanical amount, the kind of thing a camera catches and a person misses — and his jaw moved once, and he came the rest of the way across the roof and sat down on the coping beside her.

Close. Closer than a polite man would have sat. His hip a hand's width from her knee, one boot up on the stone, his weight forward on his forearms.

"So," he said. "You're in a napkin."

"It's Aoyama."

"It's a napkin from Aoyama."

"Four hundred thousand yen."

He looked out at the city and let that sit. "For that money you'd think they'd finish it."

Minako laughed, head back against the anchor, throat bare. "It's finished. It's cut to move. There's a whole engineering discipline in this napkin, you'd like it. Bias, French seams, it hangs off two points and gravity does the rest."

"Gravity's doing a lot tonight."

"Gravity's earning." She let her right knee fall open an inch. "You looked."

"You built it to be looked at." He said it without heat and without apology, still watching the towers. "Took a lot of trouble. Heels by the door so I'd know you were comfortable. Bow in your hand so I'd know you were off duty. That's a set."

Something in her chest tipped over.

"That's rude," she said.

"That's true."

"It can be both, that's the crime." She flicked the bow at him and it bounced off his shoulder into his lap and he handed it back without looking. "Fine. Yes. It's a set. Everything I do is a set, I've been doing sets since I was thirteen, at this point my resting face is a set. You want the honest version? I wanted you to walk out that door and find something easy. You've had a bucket in your hands since nine."

"Ten-forty. Before that it was a ceiling."

"There. Exactly." She gestured at him, all of him, the chalk and the wet boots. "A man carrying a ceiling. So I built you a nice thing to walk into, and I did a very good job, and you clocked the whole apparatus in four seconds like a guy checking a serial number."

"I liked it," Byl said.

Minako's mouth opened and nothing came out of it.

"The set," he said. "It's a good set. I've just got a bad habit of seeing how things are put together." He rubbed the plaster line out of his eyebrow with the back of his wrist and finally turned his head and looked at her properly, and there it was again — the widening, smaller this time, and a muscle going in his cheek. "And for the record, if you're fishing: yeah. You're the best-looking thing I've seen with my own eyes, and I'm counting the one on the roof with the violin."

"There was one with a violin?"

"There's always one with a violin."

She laughed so hard she had to catch the coping with her hand.

Lucy shoved a skull under her other hand, hard, insistent, and Minako started scratching without deciding to — behind the ear, into the ruff, the thick warm fur of a dog who had come up nine flights of stairs and wasn't breathing hard. The golden sat down against the parapet with her shoulder pressed into Minako's hanging calf and let out a long sigh through her nose.

They talked. It came easy and stayed easy: the bucket, the dead building, the manager who'd wanted the ceiling patched with the water still running. Her shoot in July, eleven setups, think about freedom. The gas station and her opinions and the tweezers. He told her about a chiller he'd jumpered with a welded contactor, and she told him about a stylist who'd taped her tits into a dress with duct tape and then charged the client for the tape, and he said that was the most honest thing he'd heard about her industry.

And somewhere in the middle of it, she watched something come off him.

It happened in his shoulders. The set of them changed — dropped maybe a centimeter, rolled back — and the thing he wore in his face all the time, the thing that read as tired and wasn't, the thing that read as bored and definitely wasn't, went quiet. He laughed twice. The second time he did it with his head down and his hand over his mouth, like a man who'd been caught, and she had to look at the city for a second because of what it did to her.

He's enjoying this, she thought. He came here off a bucket at eleven at night covered in somebody's ceiling and he's enjoying this, and it's costing him something to let himself.

The wind turned and brought the park up over the parapet, wet leaves and cut grass and diesel underneath.

"I shouldn't be here," Minako said.

Byl didn't say anything.

"I have a boyfriend." She kept scratching the dog. "Ryō. Five months, which is my record, and before you ask, yes, he's good. He's actually good, that's not a line. He's twenty-six, he's at the talent division, he's — God, he's handsome, he's got that jaw people pay for. He built me a spreadsheet. Three tabs. He color-codes my career at his desk after everyone's gone home and he drove to Saitama in the rain in March because I texted him one sad word." Her hand stopped in Lucy's fur. "He's never once been late. He's never lied to me. He has never in five months made me feel unsafe for a single second."

"Sounds like a good man."

"He is a good man." The lights went on lying down and glittering. "He's a perfect boyfriend. That's what he is, he's perfect, he's the thing I said I wanted out loud to my friends in an actual sentence, and he's at home right now asleep because sleep is at eleven-twenty. And I'm sitting on a ledge in Yoyogi with my dress around my hips at midnight talking to a man I met in an alley."

The condensers cycled. One kicked, the other didn't.

Byl put his hands on his knees.

"Then I should go," he said, and he moved.

Her hand came off the dog and caught his wrist.

It was fast — faster than a woman in a napkin from Aoyama had any business being — and it closed, and it held, and for a quarter of a second Minako let out the thing she kept in a box behind her sternum, because the alternative was letting him stand up. She felt her fingers sink past the give of skin into the cable of him. She felt his pulse stop being a thing that circulated and start being a thing she had trapped. His whole arm locked. Two hundred and thirty pounds of man came up off the stone three inches and was put back down like a bag of rice on a shelf, and the tendon in the back of his hand jumped, and he made a small surprised sound in his throat.

"Don't leave," she said. "Please."

Lucy whined — high, thin, two notes, right at her hip.

A parapet. Not this one.

White stone, waist-high, carved along the top with the running moonflower pattern that ran along everything, warm under her palms the way stone is warm when the day was never hot. The air the palace kept, which never moved unless somebody wanted it to — and somebody did, because a thread of it came up off the water gardens below smelling of wet jasmine and cold rock.

Beneath her, the lower city lying down and glittering. Lamps in the terraces, lamps down the switchback road, lamps strung across the bridge over the dry canal, all of them that steady white that never guttered, all the way out to where the wall ended and the dust sea started and went on silver and dead to the edge of everything.

Overhead, Earth. Swollen, blue-white, three-quarters lit, with weather on it — a long white comma of storm turning slowly over an ocean she'd never touch. The terminator cutting through a continent. She'd been able to find that storm again the next night, and the next, because it kept turning.

She is fourteen.

She knows it in her body before she knows it anywhere else: the elbows, the two inches of height she'd put on in one spring that nothing else had caught up to yet, the orange sash tied wrong because she'd done it herself out of spite. Sandals kicked off behind her at the base of the column. Her bare feet on warm white stone. A hairpin in her fist — the long one with the citrine head — held point-out, the way you hold a thing you are on your way somewhere to use.

Below, at the lower gate, the courtyard is full: a column forming up in the dark, kit bags going up a ramp, a ship's lamps lit, men calling to each other in the flat cheerful voices men use at that hour. Leaving at dawn. She has been up on this parapet for two hours because from here you can see the gate.

There is a golden retriever sitting against her hip.

Exactly this dog. Not a dog like this dog. Broad skull, one ear turned inside out from running, the whole heavy weight of her shoulder leaned into the side of Minako's leg like a thing set down and told to stay — the same as she is doing right now, tonight, nine floors over Yoyogi. Her eyes in the lamplight are dark amber, plain and warm, ordinary dog's eyes.

The dog whines. High, thin, two notes.

And fourteen-year-old Minako Aino turns her head to her left and says, out loud:

"Don't leave. Please."

The memory is perfect. That's the obscenity of it. She can feel the citrine head of the pin digging into her palm. She can smell the jasmine. She can hear her own voice crack on the second word, young and stupid and naked in a way she has spent eleven years since making sure no microphone ever catches — and she knows, with the certainty of a professional looking at her own footage, that she meant it with every single thing she had.

And there is no one there.

Empty stone. The colonnade behind her going off into shadow, the lamps, the storm turning on the Earth. A fourteen-year-old girl alone on a wall at the middle of the night, talking to the air, with somebody's dog.

Somebody's dog.

She has never owned a dog. Artemis hates dogs. Artemis has hated dogs for a thousand years and has never once been able to tell her why.

So she does what she was built to do, which is to look for the thing that isn't there — and she quarters the memory the way she'd quarter a dark street, left to right, high to low.

The space to her left is not dark. It is not blurred, and it does not slide away from her. It has been finished. There's stone where the stone should be and lamplight lying on it at the correct angle and the moonflower carving running unbroken through it, and it is exactly, precisely, the width of a big man standing at a parapet with his forearms on the coping, and it is the only place in the whole memory she cannot smell anything.

Like a photograph somebody cut a figure out of with a scalpel and then handed to a retoucher good enough to paint the background back in.

Her hand is still holding the pin point-out. She was on her way down to the fourth column of the east colonnade. She had a reason. The reason is gone and the errand isn't — she can feel the want of it in her wrist, the little physical itch of a task, and behind it nothing at all.

Lucille.

The name came up out of her like a body surfacing. She had never heard it, not in this life, not once. She knew it the way she knew her own measurements.

Somebody had been in her head. The work was old and the work was very, very good, and whoever had done it had come to a fourteen-year-old girl on a wall at midnight and taken out the only other person there and left her the dog, the lamps, the storm, and the sound of her own voice begging.

"— nako. Minako. Hey."

She came back with her fingers still locked around his wrist and her whole arm shaking with the strain of a grip she'd forgotten she was making, and the breeze cold in the wet at her lashes, and Lucy standing now, front paws on the coping, nose against her ribs.

Minako let go.

There were four white marks on his forearm, pressed down into the muscle, filling slowly back to red. He looked at them. He looked at her face. He didn't ask, and he didn't rub his arm, and he didn't move away.

"You," Byl said slowly, "have got a few things to sort out."

"Yeah." Her voice came out in pieces. She cleared it and tried again and got most of it back. "Yeah. I do. I've got — you have no idea, you have absolutely no idea how much I've got to sort out, and I can't do any of it tonight, and I can't do it here, and I definitely can't do it out loud."

"Then—"

"But that doesn't mean I can't pretend." She turned on the coping to face him, one knee coming up, the silk doing whatever it wanted. "For a little while. Just — for the length of a roof, I get to sit up here with a man who tells me the truth about my own dress, and I don't have to be five months into anything, and I don't have to be anybody's set, and I don't have to know what's wrong with my own head." She pressed her lips together hard. "Stay. Please. Just a little longer, while I figure out what I actually want. That's all I'm asking for and I know exactly how unfair it is."

He looked at the city for a long moment. He had the jaw of a man about to do the correct thing and leave.

Lucy whined.

Byl exhaled through his nose, and sat back down on the coping, closer than before, his shoulder warm against her arm. He took her hand off her knee and turned it palm-up in both of his — hands like split firewood, the knuckles still scabbed from the alley, ceiling dust in the creases — and put his thumb into the meat below her own thumb where the grip had torn something, and began, with real care, to work it out of her.

Neither of them said anything for a while. The condensers cycled. The dog lay down across both their feet.

"I'm going to ask you one thing," Byl said finally, thumb moving in slow circles, eyes on her hand instead of her face. "Then I'll shut up and we'll look at the lights as long as you want."

"Ask."

"That perfect man who's asleep right now." His thumb stopped. "Can you tell him the truth? Any of it — this roof, whatever just happened to you, the thing you're not saying out loud. Sit him down tomorrow and say it, and have him still be there after." He lifted his eyes to hers and held them, and there was something in his face she hadn't seen on him before, something young and badly hidden and hungry. "Because if you can't — if the answer's no — is that what you want the rest of your life to look like?"
